#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "5da3337e-31d2-5f2d-b236-60088b4bf64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3524" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "8877f6b8-8914-5022-9ec6-d4e8e0d3d159" :question/math-academy-id "e-9992" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a9cefb8-a0e4-5b79-8c26-5ac76834bb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181417} {:answer/id #uuid "b8927739-bda7-5c7d-9801-ca342ae0aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181418} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cf2677-bb1c-5019-88a5-62130e41078a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181419} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb88dbe-2a14-5a9b-a7a2-c04f09113dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181420} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb9827b-2023-5742-96d5-5dadfc0ba955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181421}] :answer-field/id #uuid "82513cd4-0854-5641-b6b1-efbbfd7c3b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181416}] :question/id #uuid "a50a050b-9ee8-550e-9478-f50c032731af" :question/math-academy-id "q-1774" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u =-\\frac{24}{25}, \\cos u =-\\frac{7}{25}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5116a423-b446-57a3-8da2-be77e9442cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339870} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8fa4ef-2acd-5a1e-8486-2335929cdcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{74}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340379} {:answer/id #uuid "a944a8d9-e058-59d2-86db-62cd8d3ee107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340669} {:answer/id #uuid "d16bd773-8156-5744-b6b9-91c9bd46d5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340986} {:answer/id #uuid "fc682f3a-332d-52ce-8dee-73e4f392d88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5116a423-b446-57a3-8da2-be77e9442cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339870} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e771ed-b822-507c-a459-71c3773ee7d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f39d2c0-c3fb-5d82-bb15-c8596eec6f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-74457" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
